From b2446c6f59faca8e49ce561f8c58b1ed675ca1c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 11:36:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 287: is there a second layer of ink, or only the first one
 averaged - a free probe

The double-tape proposal turns out to be pointing at a hole nobody had
written down. ctx_fp(T) = norm(mean over words of fp(word)). Today's upper
layer is not a layer at all, it is the lower ink averaged - and the
consequence is that the stack is order-blind TWICE. fp pools characters
before the feed-forward, so a word is a character histogram and anagrams
collide bit-exactly. The mean over words then discards word order, so "the
dog bit the man" and "the man bit the dog" are the same point on this tape.

So the question is worth asking, and it can be answered before anything is
built, with nothing trained. If an upper ink is a function of the lower one,
"semantics of the chunk" has nowhere to live whatever it is called. A chunk
has exactly two things its bag of words does not: adjacency, and corpus
co-occurrence. This tests the first because it needs no new machinery -
U(T) = mean over adjacent k-word spans of fp(span), same frozen encoder, same
characters, only the place where the sum happens is different. fp("was born")
is the encoder applied to the combined characters and is not any function of
fp("was") and fp("born"), because the feed-forward sits between the histogram
and the output.

Four measurements, ordered so the cheapest kill comes first:

  1. Shuffling the words inside each text must leave the lower ink
     bit-identical - a wiring proof, because if it moves this file is
     measuring something other than what it claims - and must move the upper
     one. If the upper does not move either, there is no order content and
     the idea is over in one number.
  2. Spearman between the two cosines: at 1 the upper ink is the lower one
     wearing a hat.
  3. The discrimination the tape actually needs, two mentions of one address
     against two of different addresses, scored three ways: lower alone,
     upper alone, and upper with its linear fit on lower removed. Only the
     third can justify a second layer, and it is gated against its own noise
     with the same 1.645 the honesty gate uses.
  4. The same residual test on a channel redundant BY CONSTRUCTION - lower
     against lower. It must come out at chance. Three vacuous gates have
     shipped in this project already; a residual test that cannot say no is
     not a test, and if this control fails every number above it is void.

Two properties of U stated before the run rather than discovered after. The
space is not in the character table and maps to the padding id, so a span is
a concatenated histogram - which is the point. And U is invariant to exact
reversal: "A B C" gives spans {AB, BC}, "C B A" gives {CB, BA}, and a span
histogram is symmetric, so those multisets are equal. U is a partial order
channel, and the shuffle control measures how much order it recovers instead
of assuming.

What this cannot say is whether the unique information is semantics. It
measures information, not meaning - unique information is necessary for
meaning to live there and does not establish it. The sufficient test is
whether the mind uses it, and 286 already has the socket: --ctx-channel.
Wire it only if this passes. Runs in minutes with zero trained parameters.

